final_datas = ["input: What are the pros to be a GDSC member?What if I am not selected?", "output: Being a member of GDSC (Google Developer Student Clubs) comes with several pros. First off, you get the chance to explore and dive deep into various domains, expanding your knowledge and skill set. It's an excellent opportunity to network with like-minded individuals, and even collaborate on real-world projects. Now, if you're not selected, it's not the end of the world! There are always other avenues to explore and opportunities to seize. You can still engage with the community, attend events, and enhance your skills independently. Don't forget, resilience and adaptability are key in the world of tech! ", "input: Can we get early placement offer from Google through GDSC?", "output: Absolutely! Being a member of GDSC doesn't guarantee an early placement offer from Google, but it can certainly be a stepping stone. The key lies in leveraging the opportunities and resources GDSC provides to enhance your skills and knowledge. GDSC offers a platform to develop technical and soft skills, work on real-world projects, and connect with like minded people. If you consistently work hard on improving your skills, understanding Google's expectations, and staying updated with industry trends, you increase your chances of getting noticed by Google or other top companies. It's about using GDSC as a means to build a strong foundation, gain valuable experience, and make connections within the tech community. Early placement offers often come to those who not only excel in their technical abilities but also demonstrate a passion for learning and contributing to the community. So, while GDSC itself may not guarantee an offer, the skills and experiences gained through active participation can significantly enhance your prospects.", "input: Will AI replace our jobs ?", "output: AI is certainly transforming the job market, but whether it comletely replaces jobs is a nuanced and ongoing debate. While AI has the potential to automate certain tasks, it also creates new opportunities and job roles. Jobs that involve creativity, critical thinking, emotional intelligence, and complex problem-solving are less likely to be fully automated. Instead of replacing jobs, AI often complements human work by handling routine and repetitive tasks, allowing humans to focus on more strategic, creative, and interpersonal aspects of their work. The key is adaptation—acquiring skills that complement AI and staying agile in a rapidly changing job landscape. So, while AI may reshape industries and job roles, it's more about evolution and adaptation than outright replacement. The future of work will likely involve a collaboration between humans and AI to achieve more efficient and innovative outcomes.", "input: Any free paltform to start full stack web development?", "output: YouTube is a great free platform to start learning full-stack web development.", "input: Can beginners be a part of this club ?", "output: Absolutely! Google Developer Student Clubs (GDSC) often welcome beginners with open arms. These clubs are designed to foster a learning environment for students at all levels, from beginners to advanced. Being a part of GDSC can be a fantastic opportunity for beginners to dive into the world of technology, gain hands-on experience, and connect with like-minded individuals. Many GDSC chapters organize workshops, events, and projects that cater to different skill levels, ensuring that beginners have the chance to learn and grow. It's a supportive community where you can ask questions, seek guidance, and collaborate with others on exciting projects. So, if you're a beginner with a passion for technology, don't hesitate to explore and join a GDSC. It's a great way to kickstart your journey in the tech world! ", "input: How can I participate in GDSC events?", "output: Even if you're not a member of GDSC, you're welcome to attend its workshops. The key is to stay actively engaged with the information shared on GDSC JSSATEN's Instagram handle. This platform serves as a hub for all event-related details, including workshops. By being active on Instagram and following GDSC JSSATEN, you can access information about upcoming events and workshops, allowing you to participate and benefit from the valuable learning experiences offered by GDSC. It's a great way to stay connected with the community and make the most of the opportunities provided by GDSC, even if you're not a formal member.", "input: How to get in web domain in GDSC?", "output: To get into the web domain in GDSC, start by choosing your focus—frontend, backend, or full stack. For frontend, grasp the basics of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, then delve into a framework like React.js. If backend is your vibe, dive into APIs, Node.js, and a database of your choice. Full stack? Well, that's your all-access pass, so make sure you know the ins and outs of both frontend and backend technologies. ", "input: Criteria to get into Design Club.", "output: In GDSC, we hire purely based on talent and skills. So if you think you have that creative mind that makes good designs and can design user friendly interfaces and system, you will have no problems getting into our design club. Just make sure you have something to showcase your skills such as portfolios or some past projects.", "input: How to participate in Hackathon and attend tech meetups and will this increase my chance in recruitments?", "output: Want to get into hackathons and tech meetups? Easy—just be active in the community and keep your eyes peeled for opportunities. Engage online, follow tech groups, and jump on any chance that pops up. It's not just about showing up; it's about being on the lookout for those doors that could open up awesome opportunities in recruitment.", "input: Is UI design an emerging field?", "output: Technology is an constantly emerging field, every device, every interface gets updates every year and we are constantly trying to improve human-machine interfaces. There is an ever-growing list of websites and apps that require UI design to improve user experience. In addition to that, we are in an era where AR/VR are emerging and those will require unique solutions to UI design as well. So in a nutshell, yes UI design is an emerging field and is certainly not going anywhere in the near future.", "input: I literally like to create my own different logic of some basic or average programs but I often fail to make people understand that logic inspite of being correct. What should I do?", "output: If you're struggling to make people understand your logic in programming, try breaking it down into smaller steps, use clear comments and variable names, and consider providing examples or illustrations. Seeking feedback from others and practicing empathy by considering their perspective can also help. Remember, effective communication is a skill that improves with practice, so keep refining your approach and finding ways to make your logic more accessible to others.", "input: How to create resume and portfolio?", "output: Creating an effective resume and portfolio is crucial for showcasing your skills and experiences. Include your LinkedIn profile , a separate section showcasing your key projects having detailed information about your projects with screenshots, descriptions, and links to the GitHub repositories. Provide a brief introduction about yourself, your interests, your educational background , and what motivates you. Highlight leadership roles, involvement in clubs or societies, and any relevant extracurricular activities.", "input: Can we apply for more than one domain?", "output: GDSC consists of five clubs - Programming, Web, Android, Design and Machine Learning. At the time of recruitments, you need to choose one among these clubs for applying but once you get recruited, you will get chance to broaden your experience and explore multiple domain without any restrictions along with guidance of your seniors. ", "input: How many rounds are there in recruitments?", "output: There are commonly three to four rounds in the entire process of recruitments. Firstly , you need to clear the aptitude round which is often the initial screening round, designed to assess a your basic skills, problem-solving ability, and domain-specific knowledge. Candidates who clear the aptitude round will be given a practical task or contest related to their specific domain or role. Successful candidates from the task assignment phase proceed to the technical interview round where you need to show your skills as well as portfolio. The HR round is the final step in the recruitment process which covers the candidate's background, career goals, work style, and their ability to work within a team.", "input: Can day scholars also join GDSC?", "output: Yes, day scholars can also join GDSC. It's not restricted to students living on campus only . Day scholars, who live off-campus, are also welcome to join GDSC activities and events. The primary focus of GDSC is to foster a community of students passionate about technology and to provide them with opportunities to enhance their skills, collaborate on projects, and connect with like-minded individuals. ", "input: What should I do first DSA or Web Dev ?", "output: There is no hard and fast rule to start with DSA or Web Dev . Instead , DSA and development should run parallel to each other in your learning journey. Allocate time to study both DSA and web development concurrently. Since DSA involves core concepts and helps in refining logic building skills , Integrate DSA concepts into your web development projects to solve real-world problems efficiently. Apply algorithmic thinking to optimize code and improve performance.", "input: How to get an edge over others?", "output: Starting from this very moment is the key to get an edge over others . The earlier you start than others, the better you become in your domain. Try improving your grasp on the fundamentals concepts related to your domain of interest , build projects , improve your portfolio . Also , stay updated with the latest trends and developments in your field and engage in ongoing education through courses, workshops, and tutorials.", "input: When will recruitments begin?", "output: The specific timing and details of recruitments can vary, and it's essential to stay updated by checking official announcements from the society. Keep an eye on communication channels such as social media platforms , emails, etc where such information will be typically shared. Though it would be taken care that recruitments won’t clash with your exams. This way, you can focus on your exams without added stress, and it allows for a smoother transition into the recruitment phase once academic commitments are less demanding. The usual recruitements are usually done before or during even semesters", "input: How to prepare for recruitments?", "output: Firstly , choose the domain in which you want to apply. GDSC offers these domains - Programming , Android , Web , Design and ML . For Programming , brush up on DSA concepts, as technical interviews often involve problem-solving and algorithmic thinking. Practice solving DSA problems regularly. For mobile app development, focus on platforms like Android or iOS. Familiarize yourself with app development frameworks (e.g., Flutter) . If you're interested in web development roles, solidify your understanding of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Explore popular frameworks like React, Angular, or Vue.js. Showcase your practical skills through projects. Build a portfolio highlighting your projects, contributions, and any relevant experiences. Ensure that your GitHub repository is well-organized and includes a variety of projects.", "input: Best resources to start programming and crack good placements?", "output: If you are a beginner in programming , then pick a programming language like C++ or Java. Delve into comprehensive video tutorials provided by esteemed educators; for C++, consider Love Babbar's instructive playlist, while for Java, turn to College Wallah for fundamentals and Kunal Kushwaha for a deeper grasp of concepts. Also , make sure that once you are done with each topic , practice questions on coding platforms like HackerRank , CodeChef , CodeForces and participating in coding contests will further refine your skills. With persistence and dedication, you will gradually attain mastery over these fundamental programming concepts. ", "input: How to manage time and coding with college at the same time?", "output: Focus primarily on coding and expanding your tech skills by prioritizing tasks based on immediate needs. While college studies are crucial for good grades, allocate time for them closer to exams. It's normal to take a break from coding during exams, so don't stress about it. However, resume coding promptly once exams are over. Clarity comes from balancing priorities without unnecessary pressure. When tackling college assignments, prioritize subjects requiring your direct involvement. For the remaining assignments, leverage the support of your peers. Collaborating with classmates can not only lighten your workload but allows you to efficiently manage your time.", "input: When is workshops going to happen?", "output: We host workshops throughout the year on various topics and domains. Make sure to follow our social media handles to be notified about any upcoming workshops. https://instagram.com/gdscjssaten ", "input: How to start Web Dev and some resources to crack future placements?",
               "output: There are plenty of free courses on YouTube which are very thorough and well explained. To get started, you can watch Fireship's 100 second videos on HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. This will give you a decent beginner's guide on how to start your journey and start building your own websites. Slowly and gradually, you can start working with libraries and frameworks such as react. In Web Development, there are also a few subdomains as frontend, backend or if you want to do both, you can go into frontend. To really master web development, you should have a portfolio to showcase your skills. The key to get future placements and do well in your career is to never stop learning. Keep yourself up to date on new and emerging technologies and never stop learning.", "input: What is the overall process of getting into GDSC?", "output: The one and only official process of getting into GDSC is via the recruitments that happen only once every year. There are a mainly four steps in the recruitment drive. Firstly, you have to register yourself on our recruitment platform by creating and account and filling all the necessary details. You also have to choose your preferred domain here from Programming, Web Club, Android, Design or Machine Learning. To filter out the best talents, we have two rounds, Quiz round and Project submissions. Quiz round tests your theoretical knowledge in the domain you chose. The Project submission tests your practical knowledge and determines if you can put your theoretical knowledge to use or not by giving you a small task and you have to finish it in the given timeframe. If your submitted project qualifies our requirements, you are invited for an interview round to assess you aptitude. If you ace all three of these rounds, you will become an integral part of GDSC. ", "input: How to start programming, till what topics to cover, which language to choose and best resources as an immediate beginner.", "output: Programming is not just about writing code, but also the ability to solve problems in most efficient manner. You can start learning programming using any language of your choice. A popular option for beginner is “Python”, it’s syntax resembles human language and is very easy to get started with. Or, if you have some experience with programming, you can go with C/C++. Talking about what topics to cover, you should know fundamentals of any programming language of your choice and should be able to implement basic algorithms using that language. The internet is full of resources to learn practically anything. Some great resources for beginners include freecodecamp.org , w3schools.com , youtube.com . You can search for the language of your choice on these platforms. And the best part, its all free!", "input: Is there any entry fees or some periodical monetary requirement for joining GDSC?", "output: Nope, GDSC only recruits on the basis of talent and skills. Students from all kinds of background are welcome here and everyone is treated equally among their peers. We do not ask for any kind of money for recruitments and stay away from people who claim to get you into GDSC by taking some money.", "input: How to start learning design (basically the roadmap)", "output: There is no hardcoded path to learn design, there are various subdomains in design itself to get started. You can start with basics of any design product of your choice based on the skillset you want to have in the future. Whether you want to be an UI/UX designer, a VFX artist or a digital artist, you can start by learning a particular software used in that field. For example, if you want to get started with digital art, you can start by learning Photoshop or GIMP. Start with basic YouTube tutorials and by creating whatever flows into your mind. The goal is to just keep on creating stuff and you will start to be getting good at it. There is no hard and fast rule when it comes to creativity and design. All that matters is designs are visually appealing and user friendly. ", "input: If I start DSA now , will it benefit me in future?", "output: Yes, learning and solving DSA regularly improves your problems solving skills and inherently makes you a better programmer. Even if the field you currently are in doesn’t require any DSA. It will be beneficial for you in the future in whatever field you are in. It will help you in solving a wide range of problems that you might encounter in your career and also will give you an edge above other candidates. ", "input: What can we do to develop our personality and thinking skills in programming.", "output: To develop your personality and thinking skills in programming, you have to keep doing and solving problems regularly. The more code you write, the more problems you solve, the better programmer you become. You will learn everything by practicing regularly. And remember, at the end of the day, all that matters is how much you have learnt compared to yourself in the past. It doesn’t matter if your code is bad or not efficient in the beginning, it’s all the part of learning process. Don’t be afraid of failures, just keep practicing and learning.", "input: What kind of project we have to make during recruitments and how can we make them?", "output: The projects given during the recruitments are simple and given seeing the skill level for freshers. For example, if you are applying for Web Club, you need to have basic understanding of HTML, CSS, and JS. So your project might be to create a static webpage using the technologies you are comfortable with. But do keep one thing in mind, anyone caught using generative AI to create their projects will be rejected from further recruitments rounds.", "input: Do I need to know programming to get into GDSC?", "output: While it’s not an absolute necessity to know programming to get into GDSC, you should have some knowledge about the languages/technologies used in the domain you are applying to. For Web Dev, you need to know basics of HTML, CSS, JS. Or for machine learning, you need to know python and experience with some libraries. Whereas if you want to get into design, you do not need to know about programming, you just have to be creative enough.", "input: any ML study session plans for the future", "output: GDSC regularly hosts several study sessions and workshops for interested people to get started and clear their doubts. Make sure to follow GDSC on social media to be updated whenever there is an upcoming study session or workshop.", "input: Who is your creator Zypher?", "output: I am build by GDSC ML Team lead by Ashish Kushwaha, Devanshi Bahuguna and Aayush Jain. By the way, almost each members have contributed to dataset on which I am trained. If you have some specific questions, you easily reach out to our team - https://gdscjss.in/team", "input: List your social medias handle", "output: LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/dsc-jssaten X (Twitter): https://x.com/GDSCJSSATEN Instagram: https://www.instagram.com/gdscjssaten Website: https://gdscjss.in/", "input: Tell me about ML Study Jams event", "output: ML Study Jams is the event organised by GDSC JSSATEN which covers topics starting from fundamental python to ML models like regression, classification, etc. It helps students to gain overall knowledge about ML domain. The students who attend these events also receive certificates and swags from Google AI. The events usually happens around May or June but the exact schedule will vary.", "input: Tell me about HackFest event", "output: HackFest is GDSC JSSATEN version of HacktoberFest. Hacktoberfest is an annual event held every October since 2013, aimed at promoting open source software development and encouraging contributions to various projects on platforms like GitHub. It is sponsored by DigitalOcean, GitHub, and other partners. GDSC JSSATEN also hosts repositories in Android, Web and Machine Learning domain. GDSC promotes student to contribute to these repositories so that they can get an idea about how open source works. The event covers full month of October. The students receives swags like cloud credits from DigitalOcean, digital badges and swags like tshirts for completing 4 pull requests.", "input: Tell me about GDSC Web Days event", "output: GDSC Web Days are specialized events organized by Google Developer Student Clubs (GDSC) aimed at fostering learning and skill development in web development technologies among university students. These events provide a platform for students to explore various aspects of web development through workshop along with hands-on activities. The workshop covers HTML, CSS and JavaScript. After the workshop, the students are given sample web pages to create within a given deadline. The best web pages developer are given priority during recruitments. ", "input: Tell me about Flutter Forward event", "output: Flutter Forward is an educational event tailored for students interested in advancing their skills in Flutter, Google's open-source UI toolkit for building natively compiled applications for mobile, web, and desktop platforms. It is basically a hands-on workshop which is done parallely with a simple project.", "input: Tell me about GDSC Annual Recruitments", "output: GDSC Annual Recruitments is an event organised by Google Developer Student Clubs (GDSC) at JSS at our university to onboard new members for the upcoming academic year. It serves as a platform for students passionate about technology to join the club, contribute to its activities, and become part of the vibrant GDSC community. **Elaboration:**  1. **Application Process:** During GDSC Annual Recruitments, interested students typically apply to join the club by submitting applications online or participating in selection rounds, which may include coding challenges, task challenges ,aptitude round and then Personal interview round. 2. **Selection Criteria:** Applicants are evaluated based on various criteria such as technical skills, enthusiasm for technology, performance in aptitude round and task challenges 3. **Interviews and Selection:** Shortlisted candidates may undergo interviews or selection rounds conducted by existing club members. These interviews assess candidates' technical abilities, communication skills, teamwork, and commitment to the club's goals. 4. **Training and Onboarding:** After the selection process, newly recruited members undergo onboarding sessions to familiarize them with GDSC's mission, objectives, and activities. They may also receive guidance on club operations, event planning, and community engagement strategies.", "input: What are rounds of recruitments", "output: **Aptitude:**  - The Aptitude round assesses candidates logical reasoning, and problem-solving skills. It may include a series of multiple-choice questions like logic reasoning and specified domain questions..  **Task Revelation:**  - In the Task Revelation round, candidates are presented with a of tasks of specified domain clubs and designed to assess their technical skills, problem-solving abilities. These tasks may vary depending on the club's focus areas (, web development, app development, machine learning, design) and could include coding challenges, project assignments, or design tasks. For programming, we host a programming contest. - Candidates are typically given a specific timeframe to complete the tasks and submit their solutions.  **Personal Interview:**  - During the interview, candidates may be asked about their background, academic achievements, technical expertise, project experiences, and interests related to the club's domain. ", "input: Tell me about SORTED event", "output: The Sorted Event, put together by Google Developer Student Clubs (GDSC), is a four-day online event aimed at introducing students to different parts of technology and how to develop things. Each day of the event focuses on a different domains, like design, web, programming, and machine learning (ML). The main goal of this event to give an overall exposure to a specific domain", "input: Tell me about LINEUP event", "output: This is a zealicon event (college fest) by GDSC JSSATEN. It is a fun event, displayed on an android application. In this event, players will be divided into teams and they will be unaware of their teammates. Each player has to interact with all other participants to complete his team. The team with maximum or all the members will be the winner. The winners are awarded with goodies and cash prizes.", "input: Tell me about CRACK THE SHELL event", "output: This is a zealicon event (college fest) by GDSC JSSATEN. In this event, we provide a set of problems to candidates. For each problem, they have to code in the given language. They are required to learn the syntax of the language in which they have to code. The programming languages are the ones that are rarely used. The winners are awarded with goodies and cash prizes.", "input: Tell me about DOODLE event", "output: This is a zealicon event (college fest) by GDSC JSSATEN. Doogle is an event in which students have to draw Google creatively. A doodle is a drawing made while a person’s attention is otherwise occupied. The competition checks the creative skills of the student. The winners are awarded with goodies and cash prizes.", "input: Tell me about FRICTION event", "output: This is a zealicon event (college fest) by GDSC JSSATEN. In this event, the participant will start completing on the given problem statements. A leaderboard will be maintained according to which, after every consecutive level, the count of participants will be halved. The winners are awarded with goodies and cash prizes.", "input: Tell me about BLACK CODING event", "output: This is a zealicon event (college fest) by GDSC JSSATEN. In this event, the participants are shown glimpse of some extraordinary UI and they try building on some app or website. The winners are awarded with goodies and cash prizes."]
